(load "carp-input.carp")

(Project.config "title" "carp-input")
(Project.config "docs-directory" "./docs/")
(Project.config "docs-logo" "")
(Project.config "docs-url" "https://github.com/carpentry-org/carp-input")
(Project.config "docs-styling" "../style.css")
(Project.config "docs-prelude" "Action-based input for Carp.

```
(load \"git@github.com:carpentry-org/carp-input@master\")
```

- [`InputManager`](./InputManager.html) — bindings, action state, axes
- [`GLFWBackend`](./GLFWBackend.html) — feeds the manager from GLFW events

Game logic asks whether `\"MoveForward\"` is active, not whether `W` is down. A
binding maps hardware (keys, mouse buttons, joystick axes) to a named action,
and rebinding a control means changing the binding rather than hunting through
gameplay code for key codes.

```
(let-do [im (InputManager.new)]
  (InputManager.bind! &im \"MoveForward\" (Key.w))
  (InputManager.bind! &im \"MoveForward\" (Key.up))
  (when (InputManager.active? &im \"MoveForward\")
    (move-player)))
```

`InputManager` on its own has no dependency on a window library; loading
`input.carp` alone gets you the manager without GLFW. The backend is the part
that needs GLFW, and pulls in carp's own `GLFW.carp`, which wants `glfw3`
available to `pkg-config`.")

(save-docs InputManager GLFWBackend)
; Carp's save-docs hardcodes the index filename as <title>_index.html.
; Rename to docs/index.html.
(defn main []
  (System.system "mv ./docs/carp-input_index.html ./docs/index.html"))
